 plan_state:{component,offset,limit} returns a bounded JSON page of the entire last accepted model declaration before resource resolution, using declarationPage character offsets and next/hasMore. Join pages only with the same acceptedAtPlanRevision and declarationSha256; compare statusSha256 to detect status changes between pages. Each page separately reports currentPlanRevision, acceptedSourceRevisions, all current plan IDs missing sealed artifacts in needsSeal, selectedComponentNeedsSeal and the selected component's sourceCurrent/readsCurrent. Source or READ staleness does not itself create a missing-seal claim. Patches can advance currentPlanRevision without accepting a new declaration. Tool-owned database configuration, bindings and secret references are not reconstructed into your declaration. Failed proposals retain the previous accepted declaration; successful replanning invalidates sealed artifacts. This query is not READ evidence and creates no observation identity, new source facts or permission. The declaration excludes assessment: submit a fresh assessment from ALL current pending IDs yourself for any new critical proposal, and revalidate every original READ ID against current source.
 Current component capabilities contain bounded local facts: current analysis type/admission, selected recipe applicability, selected/executed toolchain identities, original capability observation IDs and the latest known build or custom-command exit. plan_state.capabilities separately includes the existing five selected recipe parameters and compact toolchain identities; these are actual prior selections, never filled defaults. sourceCurrent/rootCurrent/workloadCurrent and recipeApplicable report the existing source/root/workload binding checks. Reanalysis removes the selected recipe/tool binding even if it reports the same type. A known historical result can remain readable after replan or patch: assessmentCurrent=false means its original observation cannot support a new critical assessment. Read another current original observation and write the assessment yourself; queries never mint a replacement observation or permission. A successful command/build exit does not prove current output files, runtime paths, sealed artifacts or application health, and a failed command may have changed output. The facts deliberately do not invent an output generation or persist a command environment. Inspect the actual output and use the existing seal and deliver gates; UNKNOWN remains a hard stop without replay.
 Shared recipe preparation is scoped to the selected component, not the entire dependency graph or every ecosystem. When that component's current analyze facts support a shared recipe, use analyze then prepare_tools through the verified catalog/installer before build; an absent host compiler alone does not establish that custom host installation is necessary. Follow exact source toolchain declarations; a minimum language version is not an exact toolchain version. Custom host prerequisites still require actual evidence and independent full command approval; shared capabilities never waive approval.
+Shared toolchain preparation has a separate fixed storage policy, not the plan workspace quota: an unmatched resolved release requires an 8 GiB INSTALL image plus 1 GiB host reserve before creation. A valid matching registered SYSTEM or MANAGED installation instead uses a 64 MiB PROBE image plus the same reserve; finding an executable on PATH alone does not establish this eligibility. Matching and current toolchain-filesystem capacity remain unobserved until actually checked. These are admission budgets, not measured installation peaks or guarantees that probes/builds fit; promotion also checks actual storage demand. prepare_tools has no volume-size parameter and does not fall back to a smaller quota. A failed preparation does not authorize cleanup, a custom installation or replay of uncertain effects; choose from current evidence and retain full independent review.
 backend is PROCESS/DOCKER/PODMAN; mode is DAEMON/ON_DEMAND. Ports:{id,host,target,exposure}, exposure INTERNAL/EXTERNAL. Health: PROCESS {type,timeout,stability}; TCP {type,port,timeout,stability}; HTTP {type,url,status,timeout}; COMMAND {type,entrypoint,arguments,expected,timeout}. HTTP url must be a plain http/https URL on candidate host loopback (127.0.0.1 or localhost) using the actual service port/path, without user-info or fragment; public server/service addresses are rejected. Submit the URL yourself. ON_DEMAND needs COMMAND health.
 Every ON_DEMAND component itself requires COMMAND health and ports:[]; applicationHealth cannot replace that component's health contract.
 Original state.requirements takes precedence over repository defaults. A supplied publicUrl requires a matching EXTERNAL host port; do not infer extra public components from names or prose. PROCESS creates no automatic host-to-target mapping: host and target must name the same actual process listener port. Containers use their explicit published mappings. EXTERNAL declares intended exposure; it does not configure the application's actual listener. Browser loopback addresses target the client machine. For direct browser API calls, derive and consistently configure the actual externally reachable API URL, listener and CORS origin from user intent and source behavior. Use tools to inspect actual generated browser configuration and launch output before seal; plan acceptance and local health do not establish external business acceptance. Correct the proposal and output yourself before sealing; the platform never rewrites ports or changes a sealed plan. Derive launch arguments and literal runtime configuration from original source evidence so the declared external service actually listens beyond loopback. An internal upstream address and the gateway's own listening address serve different purposes. Local HTTP health alone does not prove external access. Resolve these settings before seal/deliver: publication receives an immutable plan and configuration, and cannot replan them after failure.
